<h3 id="tc-s1"><span>1</span>Non-Profit Educational Nature &amp; Institutional Disaffiliation</h3>
<p><b class="k">1.1. Voluntary Non-Commercial Operation:</b> The Platform is an independent, student-run, non-profit educational initiative operated strictly on a zero-revenue basis. We do not charge subscription fees, sell study materials, run commercial advertisements, or derive any financial profit from any educational content accessible through the Website.</p>
<p><b class="k">1.2. No Official University or College Affiliation:</b> The Platform is an unofficial peer-learning portal created by students for engineering students and is not owned, managed, endorsed, or officially affiliated with MGM&rsquo;s College of Engineering &amp; Technology (Noida) or Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam Technical University (AKTU).</p>

<h3 id="tc-s2"><span>2</span>Fair Dealing, Ad-Free Lecture Mirroring &amp; Creator Attribution</h3>
<p><b class="k">2.1. Statutory Fair Dealing &amp; Electronic Storage Exemption:</b> All syllabus notes, previous year question papers (PYQs), and mirrored video lectures provided on this Platform are made available strictly for private study, research, and distraction-free academic instruction under the statutory exceptions of the Indian Copyright Act, 1957. Specifically, Section 52(1)(a)(i) of the Copyright Act, 1957 and the Electronic Storage Explanation to Section 52(1) expressly declare that fair dealing for private study or research and &ldquo;the storing of any work in any electronic medium for the purposes mentioned in this clause&hellip; shall not constitute infringement of copyright.&rdquo; Publicly available lectures are stored on ad-free secondary servers solely under this statutory provision so that students can study for university examinations without commercial ad interruptions. Furthermore, Section 52(1)(i) of the Copyright Act, 1957 permits reproduction of works by students and teachers in the course of academic instruction, as affirmed by the Delhi High Court in <i>University of Oxford v. Rameshwari Photocopy Services</i>.</p>
<p><b class="k">2.2. Strict Prohibition of Paid or Pirated Courses:</b> In strict compliance with Section 65A of the Copyright Act, 1957, the Platform strictly prohibits and never downloads, hosts, or distributes any paid course, premium batch, subscription-locked video, or DRM-protected material. Only educational lectures that are already 100% free and publicly accessible on open YouTube channels are mirrored. We never circumvent any paywall or technological protection measure.</p>
<p><b class="k">2.3. Preservation of Creator Logos, Watermarks &amp; Moral Rights:</b> The Platform claims zero copyright ownership over third-party lectures or notes; all intellectual property rights belong exclusively to their respective original creators, teachers, and YouTube channels. In full compliance with the Author&rsquo;s Special Moral Rights under Section 57 of the Copyright Act, 1957 and Protection of Rights Management Information under Section 65B of the Copyright Act, 1957, we never remove, crop, blur, or alter any educator&rsquo;s logo, channel watermark, intro/outro credit, or name in any lecture, and we act without any commercial intent (Section 63 of the Copyright Act, 1957).</p>

<h3 id="tc-s3"><span>3</span>Unconditional Creator Takedown &amp; 24-Hour Removal Protocol</h3>
<p><b class="k">3.1. Direct Takedown Notice by Content Owners:</b> We hold the highest respect for all educators and content creators. If you are the copyright owner, teacher, or authorized representative of any publicly available lecture featured on this Platform and wish to have your video removed, un-mirrored, or replaced with a direct link to your official YouTube channel, please send a written request specifying the lecture title or URL to our <a href="#tc-s8">Complaints &amp; Copyright Contact</a> at <a href="mailto:edu.shield3161@gmail.com">edu.shield3161@gmail.com</a>.</p>
<p><b class="k">3.2. Guaranteed 24 to 36 Hour Removal:</b> Pursuant to Rule 3 of the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021, we will acknowledge receipt of your request within twenty-four (24) hours and permanently remove the specified video from our servers within twenty-four (24) to thirty-six (36) hours without any dispute.</p>

<h3 id="tc-s4"><span>4</span>Data Security, Encryption &amp; Cyber-Incident Liability Disclaimer</h3>
<p><b class="k">4.1. Reasonable Security Safeguards:</b> We endeavor to protect User account data by implementing standard encryption protocols (including HTTPS/TLS transport encryption and managed cloud authentication/database security via Vercel, Cloudflare, Supabase, and Google Firebase) in good faith in accordance with Section 8 of the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act) and Section 43A of the Information Technology Act, 2000.</p>
<p><b class="k">4.2. Exemption from Liability for Third-Party Hacking &amp; Force Majeure:</b> Users acknowledge that no internet transmission or free-tier cloud infrastructure is completely immune to sophisticated cyberattacks, zero-day exploits, or Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks. Pursuant to the doctrine of impossibility and events beyond reasonable control under Section 56 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, the student developers and administrators of the Platform shall not be held legally or financially liable for any unauthorized access, hacking incident, data leak, or service outage caused by: (a) unlawful intrusion by third-party hackers (for which the hacker alone bears civil and criminal liability under Section 43 of the Information Technology Act, 2000); (b) security breaches or outages occurring at third-party cloud providers (Vercel, Cloudflare, Supabase, Google Firebase, Google Drive, or Hugging Face); or (c) Users voluntarily disclosing their passwords or personal details in public chat rooms. Users are advised never to upload sensitive government IDs or financial data on the Platform.</p>

<h3 id="tc-s5"><span>5</span>Intermediary Safe Harbour for Chat, Confessions &amp; User Uploads</h3>
<p><b class="k">5.1. Safe Harbour Protection:</b> For interactive features including real-time student chat, anonymous confessions, and student e-library uploads, the Platform functions solely as a passive technical &ldquo;Intermediary&rdquo; entitled to Safe Harbour immunity under Section 79 of the Information Technology Act, 2000. Individual Users are solely responsible for the content they post.</p>
<p><b class="k">5.2. Prohibited Conduct:</b> In accordance with Rule 3(1)(b) of the IT Rules, 2021, Users shall not host, upload, or transmit any content that is defamatory, obscene, harassing, invasive of privacy, impersonating faculty or students, infringing intellectual property, containing malware, or violating Indian law. Violating posts and accounts will be removed and banned immediately.</p>

