<h3 id="tc-s7"><span>7</span>Summary of Applicable Indian Statutes &amp; Legal Protections</h3>
<ul>
<li><b class="k">Section 52(1)(a)(i) &amp; Electronic Storage Explanation, Copyright Act, 1957:</b> Exempts fair dealing for private study and research, and expressly states that storing any work in an electronic medium for non-commercial study does not constitute copyright infringement (protects ad-free lecture mirroring for students). <a href="#tc-s2">See Section 2</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 52(1)(i), Copyright Act, 1957:</b> Permits reproduction and use of works by students and teachers in the course of academic instruction (DU Photocopy Case Precedent). <a href="#tc-s2">See Section 2</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 57 &amp; Section 65B, Copyright Act, 1957:</b> Protects the original creator&rsquo;s moral right to attribution (Section 57) and prohibits removing watermarks or Rights Management Information (Section 65B); satisfied because creator logos, watermarks, and names are kept 100% intact. <a href="#tc-s2">See Section 2</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 65A &amp; Section 63, Copyright Act, 1957:</b> Penalizes bypassing digital paywalls/DRM (Section 65A) and distinguishes commercial piracy from non-profit acts without intent to infringe (Section 63); satisfied because only free, open YouTube lectures are used and paid courses are strictly excluded. <a href="#tc-s2">See Section 2</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 8, Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023 &amp; Section 43A, IT Act, 2000:</b> Mandates reasonable security safeguards and encryption practices to protect user personal data in good faith. <a href="#tc-s4">See Section 4</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 56, Indian Contract Act, 1872 &amp; Section 43, IT Act, 2000:</b> Discharges liability for impossible or unforeseen events beyond control (Force Majeure, Section 56) and places statutory liability for unauthorized computer intrusion solely on the hacker (Section 43). <a href="#tc-s4">See Section 4</a></li>
<li><b class="k">Section 79, IT Act, 2000 &amp; Rule 3, IT (Intermediary Guidelines) Rules, 2021:</b> Grants Safe Harbour immunity to online platforms for user-generated chat/confessions and establishes the 24&ndash;36 hour takedown mechanism via the <a href="#tc-s8">Complaints &amp; Copyright Contact</a>. <a href="#tc-s5">See Section 5</a></li>
